1- Observability is MELT (Metrics,Events,Logs,Traces) tool.

2- Observability provide dynamic visualization of system relationships : provides real time mapping of how services and infra components interact
within an IT env. 

3- Observability Agent runs as Daemonset in k8s monitored cluster ensuring complete cluster monitoring. 

4- Observability integrates natively with OpenTelemetry and ElasticSearch, and allows integartion with custom sources using configurable APIs.

5- Observability uses PVC on k8s to store the state of monitored components. 

6- Requirements:
   supported OS: SLES,Ubuntu and RHEL (use TLS version)
   linux kernel version 4.4+
   Helm 3.0+
   kubectl on nodes 
   containerd(prefered)/docker/cri-o
   curl
   jq 
   openSSL
   for architecture choose a profile from here: https://documentation.suse.com/cloudnative/suse-observability/latest/en/setup/install-stackstate/requirements.html#_resource_requirements
   for me now: I will used 1 node 12vCPU, 32GB each. (trial profile)
   use TLS for secure access to Observability
   DNS configured
   apply network policies if needed  to control inter-components communication
   for production SSD is recommended
   get license key

7- set a storage class for Observability on RKE2. 

8- For prod env HA deployment are recommended as switching from non-HA to HA later isn't supported. 

9- store the baseConfig_values.yaml file and sizing_values.yaml file safely as they contain admin passwords and can be usable during upgrades.

10- Observability can be installed from helm. 

11- the Agent can be installed from helm or UI. 

12- before upgrading observability you need to take a backup, suse team provid ebackup scripts in git repo for that.

13- metrics tell you when something is wrong, traces tell you where it happened, and logs tell you what caused it.

14- check used RAM and CPU: kubectl top nodes --no-headers | awk '{cpu+=$2; mem+=$4} END {gsub(/m/,"",cpu); gsub(/Mi/,"",mem); printf "Used: %.2f CPU cores | %.2f GiB RAM\n",cpu/1000,mem/1024}'

15- you can save a view for later use if needed. 

16- In SUSE Observability, a monitor is an automated background rule used to dynamically evaluate system health and generate alerts

17- OpenTelemetry (OTel) is like a universal language for application data. It gathers performance data (clues) from your code and ships it out.SUSE Observability is the detective.
It takes that data, combines it with a live map of your infrastructure, and tells you exactly where a problem is.

18- Imagine your application is a delivery truck system:
OpenTelemetry is the GPS tracker installed inside the truck. 
It logs every turn, traffic delay, and engine error.SUSE Observability is the central command center.
It takes that GPS data and lays it over a 3D map of the city. 
If a truck slows down, it immediately shows you that the bridge it is driving over is undergoing construction.

19- Unlike traditional tools that only look at one isolated number (like CPU > 80%), a SUSE Observability monitor is topology-aware. 
This means it understands how components connect to each other.It runs globally: 
You don't have to set up a new alert for every single server or container. 
You create one monitor, and it automatically watches all relevant components—even new ones that get created later.
It calculates health states: A monitor runs a background script to analyze your metrics, logs, and events.
It then assigns a clear status to your components, such as CLEAR (healthy), DEVIATING (warning), or CRITICAL (danger).
It provides a built-in remediation guide that tells your team exactly how to troubleshoot that specific issue.
